Objective:

  Capture NetNTLMv2 hashes by poisoning fallback name resolution protocols on the local network segment.

Why It Works:
 
  When DNS fails to resolve a hostname, Windows falls back to:

    . LLMNR (Link-Local Multicast Name Resolution) — UDP 5355, multicast
    . NBT-NS (NetBIOS Name Service) — UDP 137, broadcast

   Both protocols have no authentication — any host on the local segment can respond to these queries.
   The attacker (Responder) simply answers first, claiming to be the requested host.
   The victim sends its NetNTLMv2 credential to the attacker in the process of attempting to authenticate.

Commands:
bash 

 # Start Responder to poison LLMNR/NBT-NS and capture hashes 
   sudo responder -I eth1 -wdv

Findings:

  . NetNTLMv2 hash captured for jdoe when a failed name resolution occurred on the network
  . Hash format: jdoe::REDTEAMLAB:<challenge>:<response> — challenge-derived, not directly reusable

Critical Distinction
 NetNTLMv2 ≠ NTLM hash:

   .  NetNTLMv2 is a challenge-response derived value —
      it cannot be passed directly to another system (Pass-the-Hash)
   .  It can be: (1) cracked offline to recover plaintext password, or
      (2) relayed live to another target if SMB signing is disabled there

Detection;

 . DNS query failures followed by LLMNR/NBT-NS queries visible in network traffic
 . Unusual hosts responding to multicast/broadcast name queries
 . Event ID 4648 on the victim (explicit credential use)

Mitigation:

 . Disable LLMNR via Group Policy:
   Computer Configuration → Administrative Templates → Network → DNS Client → Turn off multicast name resolution
 . Disable NBT-NS via adapter settings or registry
 . Enable SMB signing on all hosts

Key Lessons:

 . "First to answer wins" is the entire attack model — no exploit, no vulnerability,
    just a race condition on an unauthenticated protocol
 .  Responder must be on the same network segment as the victim to receive broadcasts/multicasts
 .  Capturing a NetNTLMv2 hash is the start, not the end — cracking or relaying are the actual next steps

Interview Explanation:

 "LLMNR and NBT-NS poisoning exploits the fallback name resolution protocols Windows uses when DNS fails.
  These protocols have no authentication — any host can claim to be any name. Using Responder,
  I answered LLMNR/NBT-NS queries on the network and captured jdoe's NetNTLMv2 hash
  when their machine attempted to resolve a non-existent hostname."
